1. Explain a critical production issue you handled and how did you resolve it?
   ->When I had recently joined the project, our team faced an issue with transaction pagination in the development environment.
   ->The requirement was to fetch 100 transactions per page, but the API returned only 56 records, even though more eligible
    records were available in the database. The functionality worked correctly with dummy data in the local environment.I took
    ownership of the issue because my colleague was going on vacation.
   ->First, I compared the local and development database data and identified differences in the data. Then I traced the
   service-layer logic and found that, after fetching records from the database, we were applying additional filtering in
   the service layer. Because of this, the final response contained fewer than 100 eligible records.
   ->The initial workaround proposed by the team was to repeatedly call the same DAO methods until we collected 100 records.
    I explained that this would introduce unnecessary database calls and increase latency.
   ->Instead, I suggested moving the filtering conditions into the database query so that the database would return the
   eligible records directly, reducing unnecessary data retrieval and service-layer processing.

   ->This approach addressed the root cause more efficiently and made the pagination logic easier to maintain.
   ->I implemented pagination at the database level using WHERE, LIMIT, and OFFSET.

